{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And HaShem said, etc., it is not good, etc.</b> It is difficult: was it not already on the day that Elohim created man, ״male and female He created them״ (Genesis 1:27)? Further, it is difficult that He said immediately afterward <b>I will make for him a helpmate opposite him</b>, and then finishes <b>and He formed</b>, and finishes <b>all the beasts of the field</b> — which makes it appear that He wished to pair him with a beast of the field, and it is a wonder to pair man and beast. And further, that He would remove a female beast or wild animal from her mate and pair her with man — the upper ones downward and the lower ones upward! Further, it is difficult that He said <b>and HaShem caused a deep sleep to fall</b>, etc. — was HaShem not able to create for him his mate from the dust of the earth? — apart from what we asked, that we find that He created his mate on the day he was created, as the verse testifies afterward: ״male and female He created them, etc., on the day they were created״ (Genesis 5:2). And our Sages of blessed memory said many interpretations and they require resolution, but the plain sense of the verse we have not found." "<b>And it appears</b> according to what preceded from the words of our Sages of blessed memory (Berakhot 61a), that Adam and Chavah were created as two faces — according to the one who says פרצוף (a two-faced being), and according to the one who says זנב (a tail), as is found in tractate Shabbat. According to this, this is the woman and this is her structure, for at first He created her attached to him literally — according to the one who says according to his opinion, and according to the one who says according to his opinion. And in this order there is also the preparation for procreation, for you will find that HaShem said to them (Genesis 1:28) ״be fruitful and multiply״, etc., before the separation — from this you learn that HaShem prepared them to procreate even while they were in this order. And this is the woman whom HaShem separated and took from the place of her attachment in which He had created her on the day they were created." "It is also a well-known matter, that every discerning and wise person who begins to gaze at the pleasant words of HaShem — the Torah of HaShem — from the time his eyes are illuminated, knows the great extent of the attribute of His goodness, may He be blessed, who was so wise to prepare the abundance of good and its arrangement. And from this point we must investigate the cause of man's sin and what resulted from it for him and for the totality of created beings, and we find that it was the woman who was the cause of all the damage and destruction of the world, for the serpent dominated her and overcame her mind, and the man was not with her, as is heard from the verse. And they of blessed memory said (Genesis Rabbah 19:3) that the man was sleeping, or according to the one who says he was going about in the world, etc., and the serpent found Chavah alone and enticed her. And from this point the created beings would have a mouth and tongue to say: the hand of HaShem did this! — in that He separated her and did not leave the purpose of creation in its first order, and there would have been no place for the serpent to entice the woman alone. Therefore HaShem was wise and wrote, prior to the taking of the rib from the man, the reason for the matter — that HaShem's intention in it was for the man's benefit; Elohim intended it for good, according to the measure of our Elohim. And this is the measurement and meaning of the verses: after He informed us in the creation of the sixth day that He created man male and female as two faces or as a tail, HaShem saw that it was not proper for man to be alone. And even though he had a mate, nevertheless he was one and was only alone. And the analogy for this is: if he wishes to go about to do his business, she goes with him; and if the woman goes to attend to her husband, he goes with her as well — so he turns out to be alone. And HaShem deliberated that the helpmate should be a helpmate opposite him — meaning, a separate structure across from him, to serve him and for his arrangement, for in terms of procreation and pairing, this was already stamped in the first order, for we find that He commanded them ״be fruitful and multiply״ before the separation. And from this point: if this was the reason for taking the female from the man's side, there is another possibility — that HaShem could decree upon one of the created beings and it would be a helpmate to assist him, and the woman would remain in the place of her formation. Therefore the verse said (Genesis 2:19): <b>and He formed</b>, etc., <b>all the beasts of the field</b>, etc., <b>and He brought them to the man to see what he would call them</b> — for in the aspect of the naming, the nature of the temperament would be recognized, and through the medium of recognition it would be possible to determine whether the existence of a helpmate is to be found. And the verse informed us that no helpmate was found, and He was compelled to extract the female from his side and make her opposite him. And the intention is for the man's benefit. And if afterward what happened happened, and this became the cause of the woman being found alone without her husband, and the serpent struck her — there is no room to say, why did HaShem do so? For Elohim intended it for good, and the ways of HaShem are straight and the righteous walk in them, etc. And this is the measurement of the verses: since the verse wished to inform us of man's sin, for this is the cause of the decree of death and it is the surround — and this is what the verse said (v. 25) ״and they were both״, etc. — before informing us of the preparation of the cause, which is on account of the woman being separated from the body of man, which is His saying <b>and He took one of his ribs</b> — meaning, the separation of the woman from the man. And since one might object and say: why was this preparation for sin, and should HaShem not have separated her and established her in her place, and then there would have been no sin in the world? — therefore He preceded to say that it was on account of necessity, which is His saying <b>and He said</b>, etc., <b>it is not good for the man to be alone</b>, etc. And even though He sought the helpmate from the created beings, as it is written ״and He brought them to the man״, etc. — no helpmate for the man was found among them, and He was compelled to separate the female and set the man's mind at rest with her opposite him and not concealed behind him, for in this she would bear the yoke of his burden to let him rest in his place while he sends her to prepare and do his needs." "<b>And according to</b> our approach there is a sound reason in one investigation: why did HaShem reconsider His first thought, in which He created the man and his mate with him, and then went back and separated her? For the intention is to inform that the work of HaShem is good for all, and Elohim intended it for good, and created male and female together. Except that for the benefit of the man, to arrange his preparation, He separated them from all the created beings. There is none comparable to him, and there is no possibility of creating another man for his preparation, for there are many weighty reasons for HaShem not to make two men, as they of blessed memory said — and understand. You thus conclude that the entire order described in this passage — the search for a helpmate, its negation, and its discovery — is only on account of making the female separate from the male, for the second is better than the first. But regarding the matter of pairing, the preparation for procreation, HaShem already created them on the sixth day and created them to be fruitful and multiply, and there is no prevention of this on account of their not being face to face, for HaShem prepared them for coupling even in the order in which they were back to back, such that the male would emit seed through the place where it would be received. And we must investigate this: for what reason did HaShem create the woman within the body of the man literally, and not create her as a creation in her own right from the dust as He created the male? And it appears that in this He revealed His mind, may He be blessed, that there is a difference between the preparation of human pairing and bestial pairing. For bestial pairing effects the act of seeding in whatever female happens to present itself before it. And even though the verse says of this category as well ״male and female״, this does not mean that every male is obligated to recognize his female — whatever is of his species, all that presents itself before him of his species, he is permitted. And there is no designation or designation of a particular female for a male in the bestial realm, to say ״this is the cow of this ox and this is his cow״, etc. — which is not so for man, who is distinguished not only in the category of species but also in the specifics of the species, for each one has his mate in his own right. Therefore HaShem was wise to build the mate of man from himself, to say: this is flesh of his flesh. And therefore, when He warned against forbidden relations, He registered the designations of the forbidden relations toward the males, as He said (Leviticus 18:14): ״the nakedness of your father, the nakedness of your father's brother״, etc. — see, that even though the command applies only to the females, He designates the matter toward the males — and understand."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:39:07.323463223Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}